  "content": {
    "id": "p3-p5-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "5",
    "prompt": "[Factorisation of $z^6+1$ over $\\mathbb{R}$]\n\nShow that\n\n$$z^6+1=\\left(z^2+1\\right)\\left(z^2-z\\sqrt{3}+1\\right)\\left(z^2+z\\sqrt{3}+1\\right).$$",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [],
    "answer": {
      "short": "The two factors with radicals multiply to $(z^2+1)^2-3z^2=z^4-z^2+1$. Multiplying by $z^2+1$ gives $z^6+1$.",
      "worked": "Let $P=(z^2-z\\sqrt{3}+1)(z^2+z\\sqrt{3}+1)$. The two factors form a difference of squares.\n\n$$\\begin{align*}P&=(z^2+1)^2-(z\\sqrt{3})^2\\\\P&=z^4+2z^2+1-3z^2\\\\P&=z^4-z^2+1.\\end{align*}$$\n\n$$\\begin{align*}(z^2+1)P&=(z^2+1)(z^4-z^2+1)\\\\(z^2+1)P&=z^6-z^4+z^2+z^4-z^2+1\\\\(z^2+1)P&=z^6+1.\\end{align*}$$",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
    "answerSpaceMm": 18
  },
